Other taxa in the group can have marked leaves but those of subsp.&nbsp;''argentatum'' are distinctive:{{sfnp|Rosenbaumová|Plačková|Suda|2004|page=235}}

{{Blockquote
|text=Permanent silvery pattern on leaf lamina in ''L.&nbsp;argentatum'' always forms two flexuous bands along the midrib, mostly not interrupted by the lateral veins. They are distinctive particularly from the late autumn to the early spring when a contrast brownish-maroon colouring develops on the abaxial side and extends to the midrib-zone of the upper leaf side. Although variegated leaves were frequently observed in all other taxa in our study, they never matched the description above. Silvery-grey markings constituted merely separate splashes or flecks (small-sized in ''L.&nbsp;montanum'' and ''L.&nbsp;flavidum'', larger in ''L.&nbsp;galeobdolon''), not merged into continuous bands. Thus, a combination of two distinct silvery flexuous bands together with a strong chocolate-maroon zone along the midrib in winter can be regarded as specific ''L.&nbsp;argentatum'' markers.
|source=Rosenbaumová, Plačková & Suda (2004)
}}

In particular, a [[cultivar]] of subspecies ''flavidum'' known as 'Herman's Pride' has silver-spotted leaves,{{r|Plants2Grow}}{{Additional citation needed |date=October 2024}} but even though the markings are unlike those of subspecies ''argentatum'', the two taxa are sometimes confused.

The base [[chromosome]] number of taxa in genus ''Lamium'' is x = 9.{{sfnp|Mennema|1989|pages=12–13}} ''Lamium galeobdolon'' subsp.&nbsp;''flavidum'' and subsp.&nbsp;''galeobdolon'' are [[diploid]] (2n = 2x = 18) while subsp.&nbsp;''argentatum'' and subsp.&nbsp;''montanum'' are [[tetraploid]] (2n = 4x = 36). A few [[triploid]] individuals have been reported.{{sfnp|Rosenbaumová|Plačková|Suda|2004|pages=220,223–225}} The triploids are thought to be natural hybrids between diploid subsp.&nbsp;''galeobdolon'' and tetraploid subsp.&nbsp;''argentatum''.{{sfnp|Rosenbaumová|Plačková|Suda|2004|pages=231–233}}

==Taxonomy==
''Lamium galeobdolon'' was first described as ''Galeopsis galeobdolon'' by [[Carl Linnaeus]] in 1753.{{r|IPNI:447065-1}} Linnaeus himself transferred the species to genus ''Lamium'' in 1759, and therefore the correct name in ''Lamium'' is ''Lamium galeobdolon'' {{small|(L.) L.}}{{r|IPNI:60452701-2}}{{sfnp|Mennema|1989|page=41}} The latter has six [[homotypic synonym]]s,{{r|POWO:60452701-2}} one of which, ''Lamiastrum galeobdolon'' {{small|(L.) Ehrend. & Polatschek}}, is widely used.

The name ''galeobdolon'' (or ''Galeobdolon'') has a long history.{{sfnp|Krawczyk|Korniak|Sawicki|2013|pages=18–20}} It was used by [[Pliny the Elder]] in the first century AD.{{sfnp|Gledhill|2008|page=174}} [[Johann Jacob Dillenius]] referred to genus ''Galeobdolon'' in 1719, followed by [[Michel Adanson]] (1763), [[William Hudson (botanist)|William Hudson]] (1778), and dozens of others.{{sfnp|Holub|1970|page=78}} [[George Bentham]] used ''Galeobdolon'' as the name of a [[Section (botany)|section]] in 1848.{{r|IPNI:77349914-1}} Later, in 1864, [[Paul Friedrich August Ascherson]] used it as the name of a [[subgenus]].{{r|IPNI:77349913-1}} From the time of Linnaeus, numerous authors have used the [[specific epithet]] ''galeobdolon'' in the names of species. Altogether there are dozens of [[botanical name]]s that use ''galeobdolon'' (or ''Galeobdolon'') at least once.{{r|IPNI:genus:Galeobdolon|IPNI:species:galeobdolon}} Most of them are [[Synonym (taxonomy)|synonym]]s for just a few [[taxa]].

Most authorities with a global scope accept four closely related taxa, either as subspecies of ''Lamium galeobdolon'' {{small|(L.) L.}}{{r|POWO:60452701-2|wfo-0000222509|Euro+Med|IPFI}} or as full species in ''Lamium'' {{small|L.}}{{sfnp|Rosenbaumová|Plačková|Suda|2004}}{{r|Flora-de}} Other authorities accept one or more taxa in ''Lamiastrum'' {{small|Heist. ex Fabr.}} or ''Galeobdolon'' {{small|Huds.}}{{r|USDA:LAGA2|NatureServe}}{{sfnp|Krawczyk|Korniak|Sawicki|2013|pages=18–20}}

